summary_en.technical_approach:
The method, SafeVec, extracts a refusal direction from a safety-aligned reference model within the same architecture family, then scores a target model by how strongly its hidden states align with that direction under unsafe and jailbreak prompts. The pipeline has five stages:
1. collect last-token residual activations for safe and unsafe prompts at each layer;
2. define the layer-wise refusal direction as the normalized difference between mean unsafe and mean safe activations;
3. select a continuous layer window, avoiding early lexical layers and very late layers that may be dominated by readout effects;
4. use a small set of calibration models with different safety levels to map raw cosine similarities into a 0–100 Refusal Alignment Score (RAS);
5. score the target model using its alignment to the refusal direction on unsafe and jailbreak inputs.

This is a white-box method by design. It requires access to hidden states and benefits from having a family-matched reference model plus calibration models. In return, it avoids generation, avoids judge models, and is much cheaper than output-level safety evaluation.

summary_en.experiments:
The evaluation spans three model families—Llama, Gemma, and Qwen—and compares aligned, uncensored, and abliterated variants. The main findings are:
1. safe and unsafe models separate clearly in refusal-direction alignment under both unsafe and jailbreak prompts;
2. RAS correlates strongly with output-level safety behavior, with higher RAS generally corresponding to lower attack success rate;
3. after family-specific calibration, this relationship remains consistent across model families;
4. the method is substantially more efficient than judge-based evaluation because it only requires forward passes.

The paper is careful not to claim that RAS replaces behavioral safety evaluation. It measures internal refusal alignment, not final behavior. That makes it more suitable for fast screening, model comparison, and regression monitoring than for being the sole safety metric.
